   "id": "spcpt_4qRycvt2DMVqCSNT",
   "name": "Thermal Decomposition of Group 2 Nitrates & Carbonates",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Thermal Decomposition of Group 2 Nitrates & Carbonates"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**Thermal decomposition **is the breakdown of a compound into two or more different substances using **heat**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Thermal decomposition of carbonates"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The Group 2 carbonates break down (**decompose**) when they are heated to form the **metal oxide **and give off **carbon dioxide gas**\n- The general equation for the decomposition of Group 2 carbonates is:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**XCO**<sub>**3**</sub>** (s) **$\\overset{\\mathrm{HEAT}}{\\rightarrow}$** XO (s) + CO**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**X = Group 2 element**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Going **down **the group, more heat is needed to break down the carbonates"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**MgCO**<sub>**3**</sub>** (s)**$\\overset{\\mathrm{HEAT}}{\\rightarrow}$** MgO (s) + CO**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Thermal decomposition of nitrates"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Group 2 nitrates also undergo thermal decomposition\n- Group 2 nitrates decompose to form the metal oxide, nitrogen dioxide gas and oxygen gas\n- The general equation for the decomposition of Group 2 nitrates is:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**X(NO**<sub>**3**</sub>**)**<sub>**2**</sub>** (s) **$\\overset{\\mathrm{HEAT}}{\\rightarrow}$**   XO (s)  +   2NO**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)  +   ½O**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g) **"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**OR  **"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**2X(NO**<sub>**3**</sub>**)**<sub>**2 **</sub>**(s) **$\\overset{\\mathrm{HEAT}}{\\rightarrow}$**  2XO (s)  +  4NO**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)  +   O**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**X = Group 2 element**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Nitrogen dioxide gas is observed as **brown fumes** and is toxic\n\n  - This is often seen as brown fumes when heating Group 2 nitrates in test tube reactions\n- An example of this reaction is:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "** 2Ca(NO**<sub>**3**</sub>**)**<sub>**2**</sub>** (s) **$\\overset{\\mathrm{HEAT}}{\\rightarrow}$** 2CaO (s)  +  4NO**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)  +   O**<sub>**2**</sub>** (g)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Trend in thermal stabilities"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Going **down **Group 2, more heat is needed to break down the carbonate and nitrate ions\n- The thermal stability of the Group 2 carbonates and nitrates therefore **increases** down the group\n\n  - (A Level only) The smaller positive ions at the top of the groups will polarise the anions more than the larger ions at the bottom of the group\n  \n    - The small positive ion attracts the delocalised electrons in the carbonate ion towards itself\n    - The higher the charge and the smaller the ion the higher the polarising power\n  - The more polarised they are, the more likely they are to thermally decompose as the bonds in the carbonate and nitrate ions become weaker"
  }
 ]
}